-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  std_customio_linux.pcpp - Std: the CustomIO unit

  An IO is a stream behind a table of callbacks (IOInterface). The IO
  routines call through the table and keep the state: a memory IO over the
  caller's memory (read / write within its size, read-only), a memory IO
  over its own growing buffer, an IO a program defines itself with
  CreateIO, the nil handle, and a file IO in each FileMode (its file in
  repo\bin\output, its name not ASCII; a missing file and a folder give
  nil). IOTruncate shortens and lengthens owned memory, caller memory
  (within its block) and a file IO (its read-ahead and pending writes
  settled first, zeros where it grows); read-only IOs, an IO without the
  callback, a negative size and nil refuse. A file IO renamed while open
  is resized through its open handle: on Linux the file put under the old
  name is kept (in the system temp folder: WSL's /mnt/c resizes by path);
  Windows refuses that rename. The rows differ by target, so each target
  has its own EXPECT block. An IOSource opens IOs by path:
  a folder source over repo\bin (entries under output written, read,
  appended through it; a folder, a missing entry and every refused
  spelling give nil / false / -1), one over
  repo\bin\output where a `..` above the root is refused, and a source the
  program defines (its callbacks see only the canonical path). A zip source
  reads an archive's directory through an IO (by path, over a file IO the
  program keeps, from memory): stored, deflated, ZIP64, data before the
  archive, encrypted entries, names in UTF-8, CP437 and an Info-ZIP Unicode
  Path field, names no path reaches, a later duplicate; a truncated
  archive, a bad signature, a file that is not a zip, a missing file, nil
  and an empty IO give nil. Its entries open as IOs, each read whole and
  by 48 seeks from every origin against that whole: small ones (read whole
  when opened), large stored ones (direct offsets), large deflated ones
  (access points), ZipCrypto on both; two entries read in turns, an entry
  IO outliving its source, the large archive in memory behind data of its
  own, an empty archive; a wrong or missing password and a CRC that does
  not match fail (small: no IO; large: the read reaching the end, state
  Error). CreateZipFileIO opens one entry by path or by IO, its content
  checked. The archives are res\tests\std\fixtures\std_customio_*.zip,
  written by scripts\customio\gen_zips.py (never edited by hand). Each row
  prints ok or FAIL with its name.

  The Linux half of std_customio: the same program, the Linux
  expectations.
*******************************************************************************/

